---
// /docs/security — the posture, from repo facts. Local-first: memory is a local stdio MCP over
// node:sqlite, the board and Agentry's own state are plain files in your repo, and outward or
// irreversible actions go through permission prompts. The plugin sends nothing anywhere; this
// website uses Vercel Analytics, Speed Insights and Google Fonts, and says so. MIT, pre-1.0.
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/docs-layout/DocsLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../../lib/base.ts";
---

<DocsLayout
  current="security"
  title="Security — local-first, you in control"
  description="Agentry's posture: memory, your board and Agentry's own state live on your machine, the plugin sends nothing anywhere, and outward or irreversible actions wait for your approval. This website uses Vercel Analytics, Speed Insights and Google Fonts."
  eyebrow="Reference · Security"
  lede="Agentry is local-first by construction. Your memory and your board are local files and a local database, and the plugin sends nothing anywhere. It's MIT and pre-1.0 — we'd rather under-claim."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 class="prose__h">Local-first by construction</h2>
    <p>
      The parts of Agentry that hold your data run on your machine. There is no Agentry service to
      sign in to and no backend that sees your work:
    </p>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><strong>Memory</strong> is a local stdio MCP backed by <code>node:sqlite</code> — a file on disk. There is <strong>no network service and no external database</strong>; the memory server talks to Claude Code over stdio, not over a socket.</li>
      <li><strong>Your board</strong> stays plain markdown in your repo, and Agentry's own process file and lane state live under <code>.agentry/</code> beside it.</li>
      <li><strong>The plugin sends nothing anywhere.</strong> It has no telemetry and no analytics.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="prose__h">You stay in control of what leaves the machine</h2>
    <p>
      Agentry runs inside Claude Code, so outward and irreversible actions — running a command,
      writing a file, opening a PR — go through <strong>Claude Code's permission prompts</strong>.
      The agent proposes; you approve. Nothing irreversible happens silently on your behalf. See
      <a href={withBase("docs/permissions")}>Permissions</a> for how approvals work.
    </p>

    <h2 class="prose__h">What this website loads</h2>
    <p>
      This site, not the plugin, uses <strong>Vercel Analytics</strong> and <strong>Vercel Speed
      Insights</strong> for cookieless page and performance metrics, and loads its fonts from
      <strong>Google Fonts</strong>. Nothing else is loaded from a third party.
    </p>

    <h2 class="prose__h">Maturity — say it plainly</h2>
    <p>
      Agentry is <strong>MIT-licensed and pre-1.0</strong>. The posture above describes how it's
      built today, not a security guarantee or a certification. Treat the agent like any tool with
      access to your machine: review what it proposes, and keep the irreversible actions behind a
      prompt you read.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
